      "1/4-inch single-pass capacity, per listings",
      "Runs on a 120V, 20A circuit",
      "Lowest price among our picks"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Flux-core only, no gas MIG upgrade",
      "More spatter and slag cleanup than gas MIG",
      "Poor choice for thin sheet metal"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "If you only need to repair steel outdoors and want the lowest cost of entry, the Forney Easy Weld 140 FC-i does flux-core well; skip it for clean indoor or sheet-metal work."
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Welders for Beginners",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-welder-for-beginners/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the easiest type of welder for a beginner?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A MIG welder is the easiest to learn because it feeds the wire for you and lets you focus on gun angle and travel speed. Most beginner MIG machines also run flux-core wire, so you can weld outdoors without a gas bottle. Stick and TIG take longer to learn."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I run a welder on a regular household outlet?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes, 120-volt welders like the Hobart Handler 140, Lincoln Easy-MIG 140 and Millermatic 142 run on household power. Manufacturers generally want a dedicated 20-amp circuit at rated output, and Lincoln lists a 20-amp draw. A shared 15-amp circuit will trip and weaken the arc."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How thick can a 120V welder weld?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Manufacturers rate 140-amp, 120-volt welders at roughly 3/16 to 1/4 inch of mild steel in a single pass. Miller lists 3/16 inch for the Millermatic 142, and Hobart's catalog lists 1/4 inch for the Handler 140. Thicker steel needs beveling and multiple passes, or a 240-volt welder."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do I need gas for a MIG welder?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Only for gas MIG with solid wire. Flux-cored wire makes its own shielding, so you can weld without a cylinder. Many beginners start with flux-core and add a 75/25 argon and carbon dioxide cylinder later for cleaner indoor welds."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is flux-core welding strong?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Flux-core welds are strong when done correctly and penetrate well, which is why the process is used for structural and outdoor work. They leave slag and more spatter than gas MIG, so they need more cleanup. Weld strength depends more on technique and prep than on the process."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What does duty cycle mean on a welder?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "It is the share of a ten-minute period a welder can run at a given amperage before cooling. A 20 percent rating at 90 amps means two minutes of welding and eight minutes of rest at that current. At lower currents you get more welding time."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How much should a beginner spend on a welder?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A capable 120-volt MIG or flux-core welder from a major brand typically costs several hundred dollars, and premium models with higher duty cycles cost more. Budget about as much again for a helmet, gloves, jacket, gas, wire and clamps in the first year."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(Astro.url.pathname),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

