            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a
            href="#top-picks"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            Jump to the Picks
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="6.2k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#how-we-picked" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. How We Picked</a>
          <a href="#processes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. MIG, Flux-Core, Stick or TIG</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Our 6 Picks</a>
          <a href="#power" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. 120V vs 240V</a>
          <a href="#duty-cycle" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Duty Cycle, Explained</a>
          <a href="#thickness" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Matching Amps to Steel</a>
          <a href="#setup" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. What Else You Need</a>
          <a href="#safety" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Safety Basics</a>
          <a href="#generator" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Running a Welder on a Generator</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">11. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          The best welder for a beginner is almost always a 120-volt MIG machine that can also run flux-core wire, because it plugs into a garage outlet, lays a bead with one hand on the gun, and covers the sheet metal and light plate most home projects use. The decisions that matter are the power you have, the thickest steel you will join, and whether you want to buy a gas bottle. Brand logos matter less than those three.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="how-we-picked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How We Picked
          </h2>

          <p>
            We did not weld with these machines in a lab. We compared manufacturer specification sheets, retailer listings and independent reviews, and we credited each number to where it came from. When sources disagreed, which happened with duty cycles, weights and thickness ratings, we said so on the product card instead of picking the most flattering figure.
          </p>

          <p>
            A beginner's welder has a different job from a fabricator's. It needs to run on the power in an ordinary garage, be forgiving while you learn to hold a steady travel speed, and cover the material a new welder actually joins: sheet metal, angle iron, square tube and light plate up to about 1/4 inch. So we weighted 120-volt operation, simple controls and the ability to run both flux-core and gas MIG ahead of raw amperage.
          </p>

          <p>
            A first welder is also a learning tool, so we favored machines that make mistakes easy to see and correct. Tapped voltage controls, clear setting charts inside the door and synergic or Auto-Set modes all shorten the time between plugging in and laying a usable bead.
          </p>

          <p>
            We also looked at the boring parts that decide whether a welder is still useful in five years. Can you buy contact tips, liners and drive rolls at a local store? Is there a dealer network for warranty work? Does the machine use common wire spool sizes? The three big names, Hobart, Lincoln and Miller, win on those points, which is why they fill most of the list.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, we kept one dual-voltage machine for buyers who know they will move to heavier steel, one multi-process machine for people who want to try every process, and one flux-core-only machine for the lowest cost of entry. Between them, almost every beginner should find a fit without paying for capacity they will not use.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="processes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. MIG, Flux-Core, Stick or TIG: Which Process to Learn First
          </h2>

          <p>
            MIG welding, called GMAW, feeds a solid wire through the gun while a shielding gas, usually a 75/25 argon and carbon dioxide mix, protects the weld. It is the easiest process to learn because the machine feeds the filler for you and you only manage the gun angle and travel speed. The welds are clean with little spatter, which makes it the best choice for indoor projects, auto body work and thinner steel.
          </p>

          <p>
            Flux-core, called FCAW, uses the same machine but a hollow wire filled with flux that makes its own shielding. You do not need a gas bottle, and wind does not blow the shielding away, so it suits outdoor repairs on gates, trailers and fences. The trade-off is slag to chip off and more spatter. Nearly every MIG welder on this list can run flux-core, which is why a MIG machine is the safest first purchase.
          </p>

          <p>
            Stick welding, called SMAW, uses a flux-coated rod and is the most forgiving of rust and dirt, but it is harder to start and keep an arc, and it is messy on thin material. TIG welding, called GTAW, gives the finest control and the cleanest welds on stainless and aluminum, but it needs two hands and a foot pedal and has the steepest learning curve.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a first machine, learn MIG and flux-core. Add stick if you work on rusty farm equipment, and save TIG for when you have the basics. A multi-process machine like the YesWelder lets you try all of them, but most people end up doing 90 percent of their work with the MIG gun.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Our 6 Picks
          </h2>

          <p>
            We did not lab-test these welders. The picks are researched from manufacturer specification sheets, retailer listings and independent reviews, and each figure is credited to its source in the write-up. Where sources disagreed, we say so.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
