        <div id="power" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. 120V vs 240V: What Your Outlet Decides
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most beginners should start with a 120-volt welder because it plugs into the outlets they already have. The catch is the circuit: manufacturers commonly call for a dedicated 20-amp circuit at rated output, and Lincoln lists a 20-amp input draw for its Easy-MIG 140. A shared 15-amp garage circuit with a freezer on it will trip breakers and starve the machine of current.
          </p>

          <p>
            A 120-volt welder in the 140-amp class is rated to about 3/16 to 1/4 inch of mild steel in a single pass. That covers bumpers, brackets, gates, carts, furniture and most farm repairs. Thicker steel can still be joined with multiple passes and a bevel, but it is slow and the duty cycle becomes the limit.
          </p>

          <p>
            A 240-volt or dual-voltage welder becomes worth it when you regularly join 1/4-inch plate or heavier, such as trailer frames or equipment hitches. Hobart lists the Handler 210 MVP at up to 3/8 inch on 230 volts. Running a 240-volt welder needs a 240-volt receptacle, usually a 50-amp circuit for a dryer-style outlet, and adding one is a job for a licensed electrician.
          </p>

          <p>
            Dual-voltage machines are the hedge. You can learn on 115 volts and move to 230 volts later without buying a second welder. If you are unsure, a dual-voltage machine costs more today but saves buying twice.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="duty-cycle" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Duty Cycle, Explained
          </h2>

          <p>
            Duty cycle is the percentage of a ten-minute period a welder can run at a stated amperage before it needs to cool. A 20 percent duty cycle at 90 amps, which is what Hobart and Lincoln list for their 140-class machines, means two minutes of arc time at 90 amps followed by eight minutes of rest. Exceed it and the thermal overload shuts the machine off until it cools.
          </p>

          <p>
            That sounds limiting, but beginners rarely weld continuously. You weld a few inches, chip, inspect and reposition. At lower amperages the duty cycle rises, so thin-metal work rarely trips the overload. Duty cycle starts to matter when you weld long seams on thicker steel at the top of the machine's range.
          </p>

          <p>
            Compare duty cycles at the same amperage, because manufacturers quote them at different points. Miller lists the Millermatic 142 at 60 percent at 100 amps, which is far more welding time than a 20 percent rating at 90 amps. That is the main thing you pay for in a premium 120-volt machine.
          </p>

          <p>
            Duty cycle ratings are also tied to ambient temperature, usually 104°F in the testing standard manufacturers use. A cool garage gives you more time, a hot summer shop less. If you hit the overload often, slow down, add rest, or step up to a machine with a higher rating.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="thickness" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Matching Amps to Steel Thickness
          </h2>

          <p>
            A common rule of thumb in welding guides is about one amp per thousandth of an inch of steel, so 1/8-inch steel at 0.125 inch wants roughly 125 amps. That is why a 140-amp welder is rated near 3/16 to 1/4 inch and a 210-amp welder near 3/8 inch. Treat it as a starting point and follow the chart inside the machine's door.
          </p>

          <p>
            Thin sheet metal is its own problem. At 22 to 24 gauge, the challenge is not penetration but burning through. Gas MIG with thin .023 or .025-inch wire and short stitch welds works best. Flux-core runs hotter and is a poor match for thin auto body panels.
          </p>

          <p>
            For heavier steel, joint preparation matters as much as amperage. Beveling the edges, cleaning mill scale with a grinder and making more than one pass let a 140-amp machine join steel thicker than its single-pass rating. An angle grinder with a flap disc and wire wheel is the tool that makes that prep possible.
          </p>

          <p>
            Manufacturer thickness ratings are best-case, single-pass figures on clean mild steel. If most of your work is near the top of a machine's range, step up a size. Welds that look good on top can still lack penetration underneath, and that is a strength problem you cannot see.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="setup" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. What Else You Need Before Your First Weld
          </h2>

          <p>
            The welder is only part of the cost. You will need an auto-darkening helmet, leather welding gloves, a flame-resistant jacket or cotton clothing, safety glasses to wear under the hood, and a fire extinguisher within reach. Budget roughly as much again as a starter welder for safety gear and consumables in the first year.
          </p>

          <p>
            For gas MIG, add a shielding gas cylinder and a regulator. Most beginners use a 75 percent argon and 25 percent carbon dioxide mix for mild steel. You usually buy or lease the cylinder from a welding supplier and swap it for a full one when it runs out. Flux-core needs no gas, which is why many people start there.
          </p>

          <p>
            Clamps and a solid steel table make a bigger difference to weld quality than most beginners expect. A bench vise holds parts square, magnetic angle clamps help with corners, and a clean ground connection on bare metal prevents a sputtering arc.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep spare contact tips, nozzle gel and a pair of welding pliers on hand. Tips wear and clog with spatter, and a worn tip is behind many of the erratic arcs new welders blame on themselves.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Safety Basics Every Beginner Needs
          </h2>

          <p>
            Welding arcs emit ultraviolet and infrared light that can burn eyes and skin within seconds. General guidance from the American Welding Society and ANSI Z49.1 is to use a helmet with the correct filter shade for the process and current, commonly around shade 10 to 13 for MIG, and to protect bystanders with screens. An auto-darkening helmet makes learning easier because you can see the joint before you strike.
          </p>

          <p>
            Fumes are the hazard people underestimate. OSHA guidance stresses ventilation and keeping your head out of the plume. Never weld galvanized or coated steel without removing the coating and ventilating well, because zinc fumes cause metal fume fever. Weld in a well-ventilated space or use local fume extraction.
          </p>

          <p>
            Fire is the most common welding accident at home. Sparks travel many feet and smolder in sawdust, rags and cardboard. Clear the area of combustibles, keep a fire extinguisher at hand, and watch the area for a while after you stop welding, a practice the standards call a fire watch.
          </p>

          <p>
            Electrical safety matters too. Use a properly rated circuit, never weld standing in water, keep cables in good repair, and follow the manual's grounding instructions. If you are adding a 240-volt circuit, have a licensed electrician do it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="generator" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Running a Welder on a Generator
          </h2>

          <p>
            A welder can run on a portable generator, which is useful for fence and farm repairs away from the house. Miller says the Millermatic 142 gives full output on generators of 5,000 watts or more and limited output on smaller ones. Inverter welders tend to tolerate generator power better than older transformer machines, but check the manual.
          </p>

          <p>
            Size the generator for the welder's input draw at the amperage you will use, not its maximum. A 140-amp 120-volt welder working near its rating can draw close to a full 20-amp circuit. Our generator wattage chart shows how to add up running and starting loads.
          </p>

          <p>
            Use a heavy-gauge, short extension cord if you must use one, because voltage drop starves a welder and makes the arc erratic. Our guide to generator extension cord size covers the gauge to use for the distance.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep the generator outside and away from doors and windows, as with any generator, and do not run it in a garage. Carbon monoxide does not care that you are only welding for a few minutes.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying more amperage than your outlet can deliver</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 210-amp welder on a 115-volt circuit is a 140-amp welder. If you do not have or plan to install a 230-volt outlet, the extra capacity of a dual-voltage machine is money spent on nothing. Match the welder to the circuit you have, and check that it is a dedicated 20-amp circuit.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using flux-core on thin sheet metal</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Flux-core runs hot and penetrates aggressively, which is great on dirty steel outdoors and terrible on 22-gauge body panels. You will burn holes and warp the panel. Use gas MIG with thin wire and short stitch welds for sheet metal.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping metal prep</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Mill scale, rust, paint and oil cause porosity, spatter and weak welds. Grind to bright metal where the weld and the ground clamp go. Most beginner welds that look bubbly or sputter are a prep problem, not a machine problem.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring duty cycle</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Running a 20-percent machine at full current for long seams trips the thermal overload and shortens component life. Weld in shorter stretches, let the machine cool, or buy a welder with a higher rating at your typical amperage.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Welding galvanized steel without precautions</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Zinc coatings release fumes that cause metal fume fever. Grind the coating off well back from the joint and ventilate thoroughly, or choose uncoated steel. Never weld coated metal in a closed garage.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the easiest type of welder for a beginner?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A MIG welder is the easiest to learn because it feeds the wire for you and lets you focus on gun angle and travel speed. Most beginner MIG machines also run flux-core wire, so you can weld outdoors without a gas bottle. Stick and TIG take longer to learn.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I run a welder on a regular household outlet?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, 120-volt welders like the Hobart Handler 140, Lincoln Easy-MIG 140 and Millermatic 142 run on household power. Manufacturers generally want a dedicated 20-amp circuit at rated output, and Lincoln lists a 20-amp draw. A shared 15-amp circuit will trip and weaken the arc.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How thick can a 120V welder weld?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Manufacturers rate 140-amp, 120-volt welders at roughly 3/16 to 1/4 inch of mild steel in a single pass. Miller lists 3/16 inch for the Millermatic 142, and Hobart's catalog lists 1/4 inch for the Handler 140. Thicker steel needs beveling and multiple passes, or a 240-volt welder.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need gas for a MIG welder?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only for gas MIG with solid wire. Flux-cored wire makes its own shielding, so you can weld without a cylinder. Many beginners start with flux-core and add a 75/25 argon and carbon dioxide cylinder later for cleaner indoor welds.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is flux-core welding strong?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Flux-core welds are strong when done correctly and penetrate well, which is why the process is used for structural and outdoor work. They leave slag and more spatter than gas MIG, so they need more cleanup. Weld strength depends more on technique and prep than on the process.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What does duty cycle mean on a welder?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is the share of a ten-minute period a welder can run at a given amperage before cooling. A 20 percent rating at 90 amps means two minutes of welding and eight minutes of rest at that current. At lower currents you get more welding time.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much should a beginner spend on a welder?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A capable 120-volt MIG or flux-core welder from a major brand typically costs several hundred dollars, and premium models with higher duty cycles cost more. Budget about as much again for a helmet, gloves, jacket, gas, wire and clamps in the first year.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

