          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            The <strong class="text-white">best welder for beginners</strong> is the <strong class="text-white">Hobart Handler 140</strong> for most people: it runs on household power, takes gas or flux-core, and covers sheet metal to 1/4-inch steel. Spend more on the <strong class="text-white">Millermatic 142</strong> for its duty cycle and Auto-Set, or step up to the <strong class="text-white">Handler 210 MVP</strong> if thicker steel and a 230V outlet are in your future.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Whatever you buy, set aside money for a good auto-darkening helmet and gloves, prep your metal to bright steel, and practice on scrap before your first real project. Technique, not the logo on the machine, decides how your welds turn out.
